22 claims; 0 removal(s).
  run_ids: null -> ["43934d8f-bf75-474c-b935-52f825b5c9ef"]
  crate: null -> "byteorder"
  version: null -> "1.5.0"
  title: null -> "Exact integer and floating bit serialization, with explicit failure conditions"
  explanation: null -> "byteorder 1.5.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. The two handwritten Default methods always panic. "
  trusted_assumptions: null -> "F32/F64 are explicit 32/64-bit logical patterns; two executable std to_bits models preserve those patterns as unsigned words, with checked Lean value-preservation lemmas. Guards require original writer bodies and allow no other float operation. Kani checks std bit identity for every pattern and actual released writers with 6/10-byte buffers. Connecting these Rust checks to the Lean representation is reviewed, not mechanized; Rust nightlies differ. Official translation/standard integer and slice models, Lean, and Kani/CBMC are trusted. Mapped audits permit only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound."
  tool_version_id: null -> "aeneas-be86ab0f1989d04944292b345b24c435be3ad10a"
  environment: null -> "Archive-identical Rust/Cargo.toml; x86_64 default features, debug extraction. Rust nightly-2026-09-17, Charon08d7c9d50144bd84848212c24701253585e90d1f, Aeneas be86ab0f1989d04944292b345b24c435be3ad10a, Lean4.35.0. Kani0.68.0/CBMC6.11.0/Rust nightly-2026-08-21: six harnesses, 276 checks, no failures per crate."
  evidence_url: null -> "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/c730051590dce08c1ede987497d6e45efb2f839a"
  limitations: null -> "This covers 20 selected writer methods and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Reads, remaining signed variable-width wrappers, bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified."
